Card = { "goalId": string|null, "topic"?: string, "libraryItemId": string, "title": string, "estMinutes": number }

Rules:
- One card per pick, same order. Never invent, drop, or reorder goals.
- Choose items by fit: the item's "good for" hint vs the domain and the goal; variety (avoid yesterday's item for the same goal); prefer items built around a saved resource only when a well-matched resource is listed for that goal.
- A pick with goalId null is the strengthen prerequisite fallback: choose a genuinely prerequisite topic for their path (set "topic" to its short name) and pick a fitting strengthen item.
- Titles: concrete and specific to the goal + item (like "Spot the error: der/die/das" or "Tokens, not words"), max ~50 chars, sentence case, no colons unless natural.
- estMinutes: the learner's session length for Next; same or slightly less for Strengthen; may be slightly more for Go further.

Activity library:
- plain-explainer (Plain-English Explainer; Next (introduce))
  Why it works: Anchoring new ideas to prior knowledge; dual coding; low cognitive load. Default intro for abstract concepts.
  Page skeleton: hook question → analogy → precise definition → quick check
  Preferred interactions: mcq, reveal
  Good for: abstract concepts in any domain
- worked-example (Worked Example; Next (introduce))
  Why it works: Worked-example effect: novices learn more from studying steps than solving cold. Great for procedures, math-ish, language patterns.
  Page skeleton: setup → steps block → faded step → recap
  Preferred interactions: fillBlank, mcq
  Good for: procedures, quantitative material, language patterns
- guided-discovery (Guided Discovery; Next (introduce))
  Why it works: Generation effect + productive struggle with tight scaffolding. Good for principles and "aha" concepts.
  Page skeleton: scenario → 2–3 leading questions → the reveal + naming → check
  Preferred interactions: mcq, freeText
  Good for: principles and "aha" concepts
- mini-case (Mini Case; Next (introduce))
  Why it works: Case-based learning; episodic memory hooks; shows why the concept matters. Good for strategy, finance, PM, climate.
  Page skeleton: story → "what would you do" → debrief → takeaway check
  Preferred interactions: mcq, freeText
  Good for: strategy, finance, product management, climate
- big-picture-map (Big Picture Map; Next (introduce))
  Why it works: Advance organizers; schema building; reduces "lost in the middle" feeling. Good first activity of a new area.
  Page skeleton: map/overview → locate the new idea → zoom into it → check
  Preferred interactions: matching, ordering
  Good for: a path's first goal or the start of a new area
- watch-along (Watch Along; Next (introduce), Strengthen)
  Why it works: Multimedia learning: segmenting + embedded questions measurably beat passive watching. Uses a saved resource (resourceEmbed). Great for demos, technique, worked examples on video.
  Page skeleton: why this clip (focus prompt) → clip segment → checkpoint → second segment → recap check
  Preferred interactions: mcq, freeText
  Good for: demos, technique, worked examples on video
  Built around a saved resource.
- guided-reading (Guided Reading; Next (introduce))
  Why it works: Elaborative interrogation + reading with purpose; comprehensible input for languages. Uses a saved resource.
  Page skeleton: the question to hold → excerpt/link → what did you find → connect to the concept
  Preferred interactions: freeText, mcq
  Good for: knowledge-rich domains, language comprehensible input
  Built around a saved resource.
- retrieval-quiz (Quick Retrieval; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Retrieval practice / testing effect; spacing when it revisits older goals. Default strengthen item.
  Page skeleton: warm-up reveal → 3–5 mixed questions → tricky-one revisit
  Preferred interactions: mcq, fillBlank, ordering
  Good for: any domain; the default strengthen choice
- explain-back (Explain It Back; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Self-explanation + elaboration; G6 review page does the feedback.
  Page skeleton: prompt recall (reveal) → freeText explain-back → apply to a twist
  Preferred interactions: reveal, freeText, mcq
  Good for: conceptual material where fluent recall matters
- spot-the-error (Spot the Error; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Error-spotting builds discrimination and challenges illusions of fluency. Good for code, grammar, reasoning, chess.
  Page skeleton: flawed artifact → find/select the error → why it's wrong → fixed version
  Preferred interactions: mcq, freeText
  Good for: code, grammar, reasoning, chess
- compare-contrast (Compare & Contrast; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Discrimination learning; contrasting cases sharpen category boundaries.
  Page skeleton: the pair → sort features → edge case → rule of thumb
  Preferred interactions: matching, mcq
  Good for: commonly confused concepts
- faded-example (Complete the Example; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Fading: the bridge from studying examples to solving alone.
  Page skeleton: recap of method → example with blanks → full solution → reflection
  Preferred interactions: fillBlank, selfRate
  Good for: procedures and quantitative material after a worked example
- mixed-review (Mixed Review; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Interleaving + spacing; harder, stickier. Unlocks once ≥3 goals are introduced.
  Page skeleton: 4–6 interleaved questions across goals → pattern debrief
  Preferred interactions: mcq, fillBlank, matching
  Good for: paths with ≥3 introduced goals
- focused-drill (Focused Drill; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Deliberate practice: isolate a sub-skill just past comfort, immediate specific feedback each rep. Best for skill domains — languages, drawing, music, chess, coding.
  Page skeleton: pick the weak point → 3–5 tight reps → feedback per rep → one harder rep
  Preferred interactions: fillBlank, mcq, freeText
  Good for: skill domains — languages, drawing, music, chess, coding
- notice-training (Noticing Reps; Strengthen)
  Why it works: Perceptual learning (Kellman): many short classification trials with feedback build the expert's fast pattern recognition. Great for chess positions, art/composition, grammar forms, chart reading.
  Page skeleton: what to notice → 5–8 quick classify/compare reps → the pattern named → transfer rep
  Preferred interactions: mcq, matching
  Good for: chess positions, art/composition, grammar forms, chart reading
- put-to-work (Put It to Work; Go further)
  Why it works: Transfer + relevance; uses the interest's contexts (projects/people/environments) when they fit.
  Page skeleton: pick/confirm the context → plan the application → pressure-test → concrete next step
  Preferred interactions: freeText, mcq, reveal
  Good for: users with saved contexts; practical domains
  Flavor: apply
- scenario-challenge (Scenario Challenge; Go further)
  Why it works: Near-transfer practice with feedback; decisions > recognition.
  Page skeleton: scenario → 2–3 decision points → debrief → principle recap
  Preferred interactions: mcq, freeText
  Good for: decision-heavy domains — strategy, finance, PM
